Module 2 Journal

In Chapter 1, I learned about the different types of version control systems (local, centralized, and distributed), how they work, and how they will aid me in my future career working with software. Git and distributed VCS help facilitate teamwork by allowing multiple people to work on the same files, review changes made by others, and communicate your changes via commit messages. 
Chapter 2 walked us through setup. Git required a very brief and easy installation, so I don’t have any other thoughts on this chapter. 
In Chapter 3, I learned how to make a new directory with the mkdir command, navigate to the new directory with cd (change directory), and initialize the new repository with git init. The first two commands are OS commands. Every git command begins with the word “git”. I also learned about the 3 areas of Git - the Working Directory, the Staging Area, and Commits. The WD is where I manipulate my files. The Staging Area is where I will send my files (with git add) to prepare them for the snapshot. Finally, the Commits area is where the snapshots of the project are taken, and I add a commit message that describes what I did.
In Chapter 4, I learned how to make a .gitignore file, which keeps stock of files I do not want track. I also learned how to use the git log command to get an overview of the commit history. Many different parameters can be used to change how the log displays commit history. I can see changes in previous versions with the git show command and the commit name, or I can use git diff to see what I just changed. I should always use git diff --staged before I make a commit to double-check my staged files!
	Chapter 5 taught me that commit messages should be concise and explain WHY a change was made, not every detail about WHAT was changed. This helps my teammates (and me too) effectively review change history. You can change a commit message with git commit --amend if you need to fix a typo or description. Git reset can be used to remove a previous commit and also unstage a file. I need to practice more with amending commit messages and staging/unstaging files. 